  "title": "Credential Security and Evidence Handling",
  "content": "<b>Credential security protects the secrets and authentication processes that prove an identity.</b> Passwords, password hashes, recovery codes, session tokens, API keys, and authentication logs can all become sensitive assessment evidence.\n\nWeak password policy increases the chance that an ordinary account becomes a stepping stone. Short or predictable passwords are easier to guess, reused passwords can transfer exposure from another service, and missing rate limits or lockout controls permit repeated online attempts. Offline password cracking is different: an attacker tests guesses against stolen verification data without contacting the service. The defensive conclusion is to protect stored credentials and make every guess expensive, not to demonstrate cracking on real user material.\n\nCollect the minimum evidence needed to establish the policy or exposure. Do not place real passwords, reusable tokens, or complete hashes in screenshots, tickets, chat, or general reports. Store sensitive artifacts in an approved encrypted location, restrict access, preserve an authoritative original when required, and use redacted excerpts for routine communication. If a usable credential may have been exposed, coordinate reset or rotation, invalidate active sessions, and review authentication logs for misuse.\n\nEffective controls are layered. Require long, unique passwords and support password managers. Use phishing-resistant multifactor authentication where practical, especially for privileged and remote access. Apply rate limiting, risk-based detection, and carefully designed lockout controls that resist guessing without enabling easy denial of service. Remove shared accounts, limit privileges, monitor unusual authentication patterns, and protect recovery workflows as strongly as primary login.\n\nA remediation is complete only when the weak policy or exposed secret is corrected and the result is verified. Confirm that new controls apply to the intended accounts, old sessions and credentials no longer work, exceptions are documented, and monitoring can detect future abuse without recording unnecessary secret material.",
